# LordOS's logs (server/src/lordlog.h, docs/SYSTEM.md "Log files"): the root daemons' files directly in
# /var/log/lordos (lord-sessiond, lord-netd, lord-fand, admin). Daily, 7 compressed copies. Between runs
# the stamping child caps each at 16 MB (the older part goes to <name>.log.old).
#
# copytruncate, not a reopen signal: the writer is a small lord-log child holding the file with
# O_APPEND for its daemon's whole life; after the truncation it simply goes on at the start. The price
# is that lines written between the copy and the truncation are lost, which is fine for these logs.
#
# Nothing here for /var/log/lordos/users/<user>: those folders (and their apps/ and web/) are the
# user's, and logrotate works on path strings, so a user could swap a subfolder for a symlink while it
# runs and make root create its copies elsewhere. Their files are capped by their own writers, running
# as that user (lordlog_cap), and the previous session's are kept as <name>.log.prev at login.
/var/log/lordos/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
}
